              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "This quote is completed as follows:\n\n> **\"...those owned by wealthy rulers.\"**\n\n### Origin and Context\nThis statement is a famous observation by the 14th-century North African polymath, sociologist, and historian **Ibn Khaldun** (1332\u20131406). It is found in his monumental work, ***The Muqaddimah*** (written in 1377). \n\nIbn Khaldun is often regarded as a pioneering father of modern economics, sociology, and demography. Centuries before Adam Smith published *The Wealth of Nations*, Ibn Khaldun wrote extensively on free-market dynamics, the division of labor, the pricing mechanisms of supply and demand, and the impact of taxation.\n\n### The Meaning Behind the Quote\nThis specific quote highlights his critique of **state-controlled commerce and monopolies**. \n\nIn *The Muqaddimah*, Ibn Khaldun dedicated entire sections to arguing why rulers and governments should not directly engage in trade or agriculture. His reasoning was deeply structural:\n1. **Inequitable Competition:** When a ruler or state enters the marketplace, private merchants cannot compete on a level playing field. The ruler has the power to acquire goods forcefully at cheap prices and sell them at inflated costs, all while exempting themselves from the taxes that burden normal citizens.\n2. **Destruction of Incentive:** This government interference ruins private enterprise, discourages merchants, and eventually destroys the tax base because private citizens lose the incentive to produce wealth.\n3. **Efficiency vs. Bureaucracy:** Ibn Khaldun asserted that commerce requires discipline, acute attention to detail, and careful management\u2014qualities naturally developed by professional, \"responsible and organized merchants.\" Ruling elites, accustomed to luxury, lack the specialized organizational skills and competitive drive required for sustainable business. \n\nTherefore, he concluded that market forces driven by private, disciplined merchants will ultimately prove more resilient and successful than state-run enterprises backed merely by the arbitrary wealth and power of kings.",
